libdatachannel v0.24.5: each shared mutex is locked once when it is made

MPL-2.0, like the four files above. Modification history (NereusSDR):
2026-10-16, J.J. Boyd (KG4VCF), AI-assisted via Anthropic Claude Code.

With MinGW-w64, the toolchain of the Windows CI leg and of local Windows
builds (Qt's MinGW 13.1, winpthreads v11.0.1), libstdc++'s
std::shared_mutex holds a pthread rwlock made with
PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER, and winpthreads sets the lock up on its first
use. When two threads take the first lock at the same moment, the one that
loses the setup race gets EINVAL (rwlock_static_init and rwl_ref in
winpthreads' rwlock.c, unchanged through mingw-w64 v14.0.0 and master).
libstdc++ checks that result only when built with _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS,
which these builds do not set, so the losing thread goes on as if it held
the lock, and its unlock later releases the other thread's hold. On the
Windows test machine, two threads taking the first lock of a fresh lock
together hit this in 19,911 of 20,000 tries, and both were inside the
exclusive lock in 11,669.

The first two SCTP transports of a process insert into SctpTransport's
instance set at the same moment. Under load on that machine, the set then
lost one of them: every write and upcall for that transport was dropped,
the association never formed, and the far end's INIT waited in incoming()
for a first write that could not come.

Each of the library's six shared mutexes is now locked and unlocked once in
its owner's constructor, while only the constructing thread can see it, so
the setup is never raced. The instance set is made in SctpTransport::Init,
under the library's init lock. cmake/NereusRemoteMedia.cmake applies this
patch to MinGW builds only; other toolchains build the library as before.
Among them is MSVC, which builds the release installer: its
std::shared_mutex is an SRWLOCK that needs no setup.

--- a/src/impl/datachannel.cpp
+++ b/src/impl/datachannel.cpp
@@ -79,6 +79,11 @@ DataChannel::DataChannel(weak_ptr<PeerConnection> pc, string label, string proto
     : mPeerConnection(pc), mLabel(std::move(label)), mProtocol(std::move(protocol)),
       mRecvQueue(RECV_QUEUE_LIMIT, message_size_func) {
 
+	// NereusSDR: lock once while no other thread can see this channel, so a first
+	// lock never races the lazy setup that SctpTransport::InstancesSet describes.
+	mMutex.lock();
+	mMutex.unlock();
+
 	if(reliability.maxPacketLifeTime && reliability.maxRetransmits)
 		throw std::invalid_argument("Both maxPacketLifeTime and maxRetransmits are set");
 
